    "Cladophora glomerata": {
        "Species (Latin name)": "Cladophora glomerata",
        "Common name": "K\u00fctzing, Green filamentous algae, Gr\u00f6nslick (swedish)",
        "Division": "Chlorophyta",
        "Micro/Macro": "Macro",
        "Color": "green",
        "Water type (marine/fresh/brackish/terrestrial)": "marine, brackish, freshwater",
        "Geographic Positioning within Europe": "north America (especially great lakes), Europe, Atlantic Islands, Carribean Islands, Asia, Africa, New Zealand",
        "Habitat": "\"Common in unshaded waters especially during summer low flow.  May be found under a variety of conditions, but reaches problem levels in lakes with a combination of rocky shorelines and high phosphate, and seems more common in warmer streams further north in New Zealand.\"",
        "Invasive?": "potentially; can induce invasion of  dreissenid mussels; blooms have also been an issue in the US",
        "Invasive information (where, how, strategies, etc.)": "",
        "Water temperature for growth (C)": "9 to 30",
        "Optimal water temperature for growth (C)": "",
        "Salinity for growth (PSU)": "A study foud that the biomass decreased with increasing salinity - so prefers lower salinity levels for optimal growth",
        "Depth range (m)": "<5",
        "Is the algae facing changes with rising sea temp?": "Yes",
        "Unnamed: 15": "sensitive to temp changes, and high summer temps",
        "Temperature tolerance": "sensitive to temperature changes",
        "Thickness/size": "filament length 30\u201350 cm",
        "Status (impacted by humans?)": "Causes a series of environmental problems so utilization is an environmetnal demand. New source: proliferates in P -rich environments then drifts towards shore in rotting masses ffrom major seasonal die-offs (increased N can also cause similar result)",
        "Already on the market?": "",
        "Listed in EU Novel Food Catalogue": "",
        "Food supplement at Union list of Novel Foods": "",
        "production cost (high or low and what does that mean, laborintensive, complex processing) ": "",
        "Can it be grown in a polyculture?": "yes - has the potential to ",
        "Where/how is it grown? lab/farm/wild": "wild - too much growth, many places need to remove large collections of biomass from water systems (esp freshwater)",
        "Growth rate + specify if in wild or cultivation (can be in terms of biomass yield) ": "high  - Maximum 0.77 day\u22121 (no units per day were specificed, assumed cm? or measurement of respiration volume growth)",
        "Harvesting/collection methods": "Somewhat difficult : sometimes attached, sometimes free floating, and its appearance/characteristics vary based on environmental conditions. new source: use of surface-flow constructed wetlands (SFCW) dominated by Cladophora remove and reclaim nutrients in diffuse domestic effluent (simultaneously controls growth rate)",
        "Time of Harvest (incl # times a year)": "biannual  - 2 peaks in biomass observed july + september (in Neva estuary, Gul of Finland)",
        "processing/extraction methods": "can be converted into biofeul via pyrolysis. New source: hydrothermal liquefaction (HTL), which involves the conversion of Cladophora glomerata into biocrude oil (biocrude oil made has high heating values). new source: hydrothermal gasification for hydrogen-rich gas production",
        "Country (that uses the algae)": "serveral asian countries",
        "Region in europe": "",
        "Seaboard": "",
        "Nutritional Profile": "crude protein content ranging from 16% to 21.5% DM, high content of essiatial amino acids and polyunsaturated fatty acids (highest found 11.7%), minerals like Ca, K, Mg, and trace elements of Zn and Cu",
        "take up ": "No",
        "of meavy metals (Y/N)": "they are quite sensitive to metals in the ocean",
        "Companies using the algae + their product": "",
        "Industrial applications": "production of hydrogen and syngas through catalytic gasification new source:  can be converted into biochar for use as an anode electrode in lithium-ion batteries.",
        "Extracts": "source of phytochemicals in various industries, including food, confectionary, textiles, pharmaceuticals, dairy, and paper industries",
        "Agriculture": "eco-nematicide for tomato plants infected by Meloidogyne javanica (it is an extract used for agricultural uses - MAYBE WRONG CATEGORY - should it go under industrial uses or extracts maybe?)",
        "Art materials + cosmetics": "",
        "Packaging + materials": "",
        "Environment": "phycoremediation of industrial wastewater. New source: overgrowing in irrigation systems and drainage canals, clogging of water intakes leading to power outages, overgrowth and displacement of benificial aquatic plants, reductions in invertebrate densities and fish spawning, reduction in species biodiversity, anoxia from decaying mats resulting in kills of other aquatic life, retention of pathogenic microbes such as Escherichia coli Migula and enteroocci fecal bacteria as a potential human health hazard, negative effects on recreational use of affected shoreline and nearshore areas, and depressed property values of adjacent homes.",
        "Carrier of other organisms?": "",
        "Human consumption": "",
        "Medicinal": "synthesis of silver nanoparticles with potential anti-cancer and anti-tumor activities. New source: consumed by humans in several Asian countries as part of traditional medicine preparations",
        "Risks + dangers": "blooms that move shoreward attract gulls which add fecal material to the rotting algae. high densities of E. coli and other fecal bacteria have been documentated in decomposing mats posing potential human threat (in Great lakes). New source: has been found to harbor various pathogens that pose potential risk to humans who cosume the algae (Shiga toxin-producing Escherichia coli (STEC), Salmonella, Shigella, and Campylobacter). New source: AFFECTS INCL:  overgrowing irrigation systems and drainage canals, clogging of water intakes leading to power outages, overgrowth, and displacement of beneficial aquatic plants, reductions in invertebrate densities and fish spawning, reduction in species biodiversity, anoxia from decaying mats resulting in kills of other aquatic life, retention of pathogenic microbes such as Escherichia coli Migula and enteroocci fecal bacteria as a potential human health hazard, negative effects on recreational use of affected shoreline and nearshore areas, and depressed property values of adjacent homes.",
        "usage within agriculture + products": "production of hydrogen and syngas through catalytic gasification. New source: eco-nematicide for tomato plants infected by Meloidogyne javanica. new source: phycoremediation of industrial wastewater. new source:  can be converted into biochar for use as an anode electrode in lithium-ion batteries. new source: source of phytochemicals in various industries, including food, confectionary, textiles, pharmaceuticals, dairy, and paper industries",
        "medicinal": "synthesis of silver nanoparticles with potential anti-cancer and anti-tumor activities",
        "Flavor/Diet": "consumed by humans in several Asian countries as part of traditional medicine preparations",
        "medicinal/health": "blooms that move shoreward attract gulls which add fecal material to the rotting algae. high densities of E. coli and other fecal bacteria have been documentated in decomposing mats posing potential human threat (in Great lakes)",
        "Flavor/Diet.1": "has been found to harbor various pathogens that pose potential risk to humans who cosume the algae (Shiga toxin-producing Escherichia coli (STEC), Salmonella, Shigella, and Campylobacter)",
        "other": "AFFECTS INCL:  overgrowing irrigation systems and drainage canals, clogging of water intakes leading to power outages, overgrowth, and displacement of beneficial aquatic plants, reductions in invertebrate densities and fish spawning, reduction in species biodiversity, anoxia from decaying mats resulting in kills of other aquatic life, retention of pathogenic microbes such as Escherichia coli Migula and enteroocci fecal bacteria as a potential human health hazard, negative effects on recreational use of affected shoreline and nearshore areas, and depressed property values of adjacent homes.",
        "Additional properties": "",
        " ": ""
    },
