    "Dictyosiphon foeniculaceus": {
        "Species (Latin name)": "Dictyosiphon foeniculaceus",
        "Common name": "clawed fork weed. New source: Golden sea hair",
        "Division": "Phaeophyta",
        "Micro/Macro": "Macro",
        "Color": "brown",
        "Water type (marine/fresh/brackish/terrestrial)": "marine, brackish",
        "Geographic Positioning within Europe": "Occurs in the coasts of Ireland and UK on rocks and stones in shallow subtitle on sheltered to moderately exposed coasts. Also found in Denmark, Norway, and Sweden)",
        "Habitat": "rock, epiphytically on other seaweeds, and in free-floating mats. It grows along protected shorelines and in estuaries.",
        "Invasive?": "no",
        "Invasive information (where, how, strategies, etc.)": "",
        "Water temperature for growth (C)": "in a study focusing on life cycle of species in context of cultivation  preferred temperatures for stages were given:  the bialgal culture, 10 and 14\u2103-LD conditions were suitable for macrothallus formation, 18\u2103-SD, LD conditions were suitable for the growth of prostrate thalli, and 22\u2103-SD conditions were suitable for the formation of plurilocular sporangia. New source: samples were taken from wild populations with water of 6C",
        "Optimal water temperature for growth (C)": "May-15",
        "Salinity for growth (PSU)": "16PSU, thrive in less saline waters: contained lightly sulphated fucoidans - less than others in the study. The study suggests that species which thrive in saline waters may contain more sulphated fucoidans as a means of mitigating osmotic stress",
        "Depth range (m)": "0-10",
        "Is the algae facing changes with rising sea temp?": "Yes",
        "Unnamed: 15": "a study of a species very similar found that it prefered cooler temperatures (+ has low temp tolerance)",
        "Temperature tolerance": "upper survival limit: 26-27C",
        "Thickness/size": "30-90cm in height, branches eith diameter of 25mm. New source: up to 40cm tall",
        "Status (impacted by humans?)": "",
        "Already on the market?": "no - not used for any commercial purpose",
        "Listed in EU Novel Food Catalogue": "",
        "Food supplement at Union list of Novel Foods": "",
        "production cost (high or low and what does that mean, laborintensive, complex processing) ": "",
        "Can it be grown in a polyculture?": "",
        "Where/how is it grown? lab/farm/wild": "common in the Baltic Sea, cultivation is possible but only with individuals that have no sexual organs (receptacles), it would be possible to use the others as seed biomass for aquaculture but must be investigated further.  biofouling organisms are a big challenge in cultivation - dessication regualrly or in large cultivation tanks absent of biofouling organisms. New source: 2 organic growth regulators - PSIS contained in seawater or some algae and another is MIS secreted from specific yellow bacteria being parasitic on the host",
        "Growth rate + specify if in wild or cultivation (can be in terms of biomass yield) ": "Relatively slow growing  -  compared to others species in study (0.05 \u00b1 0.01 % wet weight/week - average in study was 1.68 \u00b10.27 %/week)",
        "Harvesting/collection methods": "",
        "Time of Harvest (incl # times a year)": "Not clear - one study harvested in May (in Baltic Sea/Northern hemisphere)",
        "processing/extraction methods": "Clean/wash, dry, grind into fine powder, extract usign a solvent (water, ethanol e.g. 99% v/v ethanol), extract (methods differn depending on compound/product:  maceration, Soxhlet extraction, or ultrasound-assisted extraction)",
        "Country (that uses the algae)": "Used in Europe: Norway, through the Baltic, Faroe Islands, Italy, England, Ireland",
        "Region in europe": "Mainly northern europe (+ southern europe)",
        "Seaboard": "",
        "Nutritional Profile": "Calcium, Magnesium, Potassium, Sodium, Iron, Manganese, Zinc. New source: low lipid, carbohydrate, and protein content (relative to others in study)",
        "take up ": "",
        "of meavy metals (Y/N)": "",
        "Companies using the algae + their product": "",
        "Industrial applications": "",
        "Extracts": "",
        "Agriculture": "",
        "Art materials + cosmetics": "",
        "Packaging + materials": "",
        "Environment": "able to raise pH above 9 and thus may be capable of also utilising HCO3\u2212 for photosynthesis",
        "Carrier of other organisms?": "",
        "Human consumption": "",
        "Medicinal": "pharmeceuticals",
        "Risks + dangers": "",
        "usage within agriculture + products": "",
        "medicinal": "therepeutic properties particularly in the treatment of glioblastoma and uveal melanoma (anti-tumour/cancer properties). also contains fucoidans which have potential medicinal applications.",
        "Flavor/Diet": "",
        "medicinal/health": "",
        "Flavor/Diet.1": "",
        "other": "",
        "Additional properties": "",
        " ": ""
    },
