    "Fucus vesiculosus": {
        "Species (Latin name)": "Fucus vesiculosus",
        "Common name": "Bladderwrack, rockweed, red fucus, dyers fucus, rock wrack, black tang, and bladder fucus; irish:  Feamainn bhoilg\u00edneach",
        "Division": "Phaeophyta",
        "Micro/Macro": "Macro",
        "Color": "brown",
        "Water type (marine/fresh/brackish/terrestrial)": "marine",
        "Geographic Positioning within Europe": "grows along the coastlines of the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans, the North and Baltic Seas, and various waters in Canada and the United States",
        "Habitat": "rocky shores in the mid-intertida; exposed rocky; sheltered lagoons--very variable",
        "Invasive?": "no",
        "Invasive information (where, how, strategies, etc.)": "",
        "Water temperature for growth (C)": "",
        "Optimal water temperature for growth (C)": "",
        "Salinity for growth (PSU)": "one of the few marine species that can survive in the low salinity of the Baltic Sea. New source: 6.5 psu in a study in the West Estonian Archipelago (brackish?)",
        "Depth range (m)": "3 to 4",
        "Is the algae facing changes with rising sea temp?": "yes",
        "Unnamed: 15": "Increased CO2 in the water has been known to decrease growth rate up to 15% in this species, and also lowers tissue density, making the organism weaker to currents/waves/storms. Phlorotannin content can also decrease in this scenario",
        "Temperature tolerance": "species within genus max temp 23C or 25C (23C; Fucis gardneri, 25C; Fucus spiralis)",
        "Thickness/size": "90 cm tall",
        "Status (impacted by humans?)": "",
        "Already on the market?": "yes",
        "Listed in EU Novel Food Catalogue": "yes",
        "Food supplement at Union list of Novel Foods": "yes, fucoidan extract",
        "production cost (high or low and what does that mean, laborintensive, complex processing) ": "",
        "Can it be grown in a polyculture?": "yes",
        "Where/how is it grown? lab/farm/wild": "Harvested from natural occurance in the water - very abundant ",
        "Growth rate + specify if in wild or cultivation (can be in terms of biomass yield) ": "",
        "Harvesting/collection methods": "Simple harvest from the water - taken up in nets, loaded onto boat, and brought ashore",
        "Time of Harvest (incl # times a year)": "March - late October",
        "processing/extraction methods": "development of a method: green biorefinery process for obtaining fucoidan, laminarin, mannitol, alginate and protein using hydrochloric acid + green extrraction solvent",
        "Country (that uses the algae)": "Ireland",
        "Region in europe": "",
        "Seaboard": "",
        "Nutritional Profile": "calcium, iodine, magnesium, potassium, soidum, zinc, vitamins A and C, and high in phytochemicals which may help to reduce oxidative stress. High in fiber and high in alginic acid and fucoidans. New source: good sources of fucoidan, laminarin, protein and mannitol",
        "take up ": "Yes",
        "of meavy metals (Y/N)": "in Gulf of Riga high levels of heavy metals so it is not recommended for food consumption from this area (pollution)",
        "Companies using the algae + their product": "Dalton marine cosmetics uses in its skincare to prevent premature skin aging",
        "Industrial applications": "the algae from the Gulf of Riga is suggested for bioenergy use (instead of human consumption due to high heavy metal conc)",
        "Extracts": "",
        "Agriculture": "fertiliser",
        "Art materials + cosmetics": "fucoidan in moisturizer - inhibits hyaluronidase enzyme",
        "Packaging + materials": "",
        "Environment": "",
        "Carrier of other organisms?": "",
        "Human consumption": "boiled broth health drink--used as an herbal remedy for thyroid issues",
        "Medicinal": "used for iodine deficiency, obesity, joint pain, aging skin, digestive issues, urinary tract infection, and thyroid dysfunction, including hyperthyroidism, hypothyroidism, and goiter development. New source: same effect as some anti biotics (contains a binding site for bacteria that affect humans-some), kelp tablets",
        "Risks + dangers": "May cause skin rashes, need to be cautious not to eat too much due to high sodium, iodine and heavy metals levels.Unsafe for pregnant women.",
        "usage within agriculture + products": "the algae from the Gulf of Riga is suggested for bioenergy use (instead of human consumption due to high heavy metal conc)",
        "medicinal": "used for iodine deficiency, obesity, joint pain, aging skin, digestive issues, urinary tract infection, and thyroid dysfunction, including hyperthyroidism, hypothyroidism, and goiter development. New source: same effect as some anti biotics (contains a binding site for bacteria that affect humans-some)",
        "Flavor/Diet": "",
        "medicinal/health": "Though it has been consumed across cultures and the globe for centuries for beliefs in its medicinal and healthful properties, some of the claims are made ahead of the research/science supporting them which has had some impact on the species' credibility. In other senses, the fact that it has been used for generations has made the species more reliable in the eyes of some consumers. May cause skin rashes, need to be cautious not to eat too much due to high sodium, iodine and heavy metals levels.Unsafe for pregnant women.",
        "Flavor/Diet.1": "",
        "other": "",
        "Additional properties": "",
        " ": ""
    },
    "Furcellaria lumbricalis": {
        "Species (Latin name)": "Furcellaria lumbricalis",
        "Common name": "Denmark: gaffeltang, Estonia: agaric, Finland: haarukkalev\u00e4, Germany: Gabeltang, Poland: widlik zaostrzony, Sweden: kr\u00e4kel",
        "Division": "Rhodophyta",
        "Micro/Macro": "Macro",
        "Color": "red",
        "Water type (marine/fresh/brackish/terrestrial)": "marine",
        "Geographic Positioning within Europe": "Europe (common in waters of northern Europe - Denmark and Norway) and North America(esp. Canada). New source: most areas of the Baltic sea. New Source: Estonia",
        "Habitat": "epilithic on stable hard substrates such as stony bottoms, boulder fields and rocks",
        "Invasive?": "yes",
        "Invasive information (where, how, strategies, etc.)": "Considered invasive in Northwest Spain, no other indication of invasiveness in other countries",
        "Water temperature for growth (C)": "",
        "Optimal water temperature for growth (C)": "10 to 15 (optimal growth)",
        "Salinity for growth (PSU)": "one of the few marine species that can survive in the low salinity of the Baltic Sea. New source: 6.5 psu in a study in the West Estonian Archipelago (brackish?). New source: can grow in salinities down to 3 psu",
        "Depth range (m)": "8 to 12",
        "Is the algae facing changes with rising sea temp?": "yes",
        "Unnamed: 15": "ocean acidification in the Baltic is increasing CO2 levels, and increased water temperature can decrease photosynthetic activity; increased CO2 is good for non-calcifying algae but can have the opposite for others (F. lumbricalis may only be affected by temperature in this case)",
        "Temperature tolerance": "relatively tolarant to changes in temp (short and long term),  growth ceased at 25C, mortality after 3 months exposure to 27C",
        "Thickness/size": "fronds 1-17cm tall, thickness ranges depending on part of frond. New source: full size 9-30cm",
        "Status (impacted by humans?)": "able to raise pH above 9 and thus may be capable of also utilising HCO3\u2212 for photosynthesis, may be advantageous in the context of ocean acidification. New source: Not threatened at the scale of whole Baltic sea, however local and regional historic declines have been caused by habitat destruction (e.g. stone fishing + decreasing transparency due to eutrophication)",
        "Already on the market?": "yes",
        "Listed in EU Novel Food Catalogue": "",
        "Food supplement at Union list of Novel Foods": "",
        "production cost (high or low and what does that mean, laborintensive, complex processing) ": "potential cost effective and environmetnally friendly applications (paper, New source: biodegradable seedling pots). New source: improved machinery for fast harvest and drying of large volumes, pumpting technies are being developed, cages and fences on seabed (light, strong effective construction needed)",
        "Can it be grown in a polyculture?": "no",
        "Where/how is it grown? lab/farm/wild": "Extensive growth in Danish waters - harvested wild",
        "Growth rate + specify if in wild or cultivation (can be in terms of biomass yield) ": "grows very slowly compared to other red algae, takes long time to reach maturity (5yrs). New source: One study found that the most effective medium for the isolation of carrageenan mixture from Furcellaria lumbricalis-Coccotylus truncatus blend is a 0.05 M KOH solution, and the preferred duration of extraction is 3-4 hours.",
        "Harvesting/collection methods": "commercial beds occur in Denmark where the algae are harvested with trawl nets (in the rest of europe there is insufficient growth for harvesting) which has lead to overexploitation and a fall in production. More sustainable method in Canada - dredging and raking are prohibited and onlt storm cast plants may be gathered. New source: only species from the Baltic sea that is harvested for commercial use, unattached loose-lying form is focus on for harvesting, 4000 tonnes harvested from beachcast and 2000 harvested annually, Norway + Iceland perform bottom trawling for harvest",
        "Time of Harvest (incl # times a year)": "",
        "processing/extraction methods": "Denmark is currently the cheif producer of furcellaran (genus Furcellaria lumbricalis is in), mostly processing Furcellaria lumbricalis extracted from danish waters. New source: dispersed in cold water then hydrate and swell, then dissolution which requires heating(24-27C), after cooling aqueous form will set to form gels.",
        "Country (that uses the algae)": "",
        "Region in europe": "",
        "Seaboard": "",
        "Nutritional Profile": "protein content of up to 28%, little information",
        "take up ": "Yes",
        "of meavy metals (Y/N)": "Cu, Al, Fe, Mn, and Zn. New source: a study found higher concentrations compared to other macroalgae species",
        "Companies using the algae + their product": "",
        "Industrial applications": "red color (r-phycoerythrin), New source: used as a feedstock for hydrocolloid (carrageneen is a type of hydrocolloid) production (Estonia)",
        "Extracts": "",
        "Agriculture": "",
        "Art materials + cosmetics": "",
        "Packaging + materials": "binding agent to improve strength of paper material ",
        "Environment": "",
        "Carrier of other organisms?": "",
        "Human consumption": "in water + milk stabilizers (carrageenan), jams/jellies/marmalades made with this algae as a gelling agent",
        "Medicinal": "",
        "Risks + dangers": "",
        "usage within agriculture + products": "food industry, in water + milk stabilizers (carrageenan). New source: binding agent to improve strength of paper material. New source:  red color (r-phycoerythrin). New source: used as a feedstock for hydrocolloid (carrageneen is a type of hydrocolloid) production (Estonia)",
        "medicinal": "pharmaceuticals",
        "Flavor/Diet": "in water + milk stabilizers/gelling (carrageenan), jams/jellies/marmalades made with this algae as a gelling agent",
        "medicinal/health": "",
        "Flavor/Diet.1": "",
        "other": "",
        "Additional properties": "Coccotylus truncatus and Furcellaria lumbricalis are often observed together because they usually occur in similar areas.  able to raise pH above 9 and thus may be capable of also utilising HCO3\u2212 for photosynthesis. New source: There is an urgent need for more research and knowledge building about lifecycle, cultivation techniques, and product value chains for alternative species for open sea cultivations in the region, such as Furcellaria lumbricalis. This is specifically important for the Baltic Sea since species with mature cultivation techniques and infrastructure found in other areas are lacking in the Baltic Sea. \u201cNew\u201d production systems must therefore be developed before establishment in possible in the region.",
        " ": ""
    },
