    "Himanthalia elongata\u00a0": {
        "Species (Latin name)": "Himanthalia elongata\u00a0",
        "Common name": "thong weed, seathong, sea spaghetti, thongweed, sea bean",
        "Division": "Phaeophyta",
        "Micro/Macro": "Macro",
        "Color": "brown, yellow-green",
        "Water type (marine/fresh/brackish/terrestrial)": "marine",
        "Geographic Positioning within Europe": "Norway, Britain, Ireland, north west coast of France, northern Spain and Portugal. New source: commonly found in the Baltic, North Sea, and north-eastern Atlantic from Scandinavia to Portugal and Ireland. I",
        "Habitat": "rocks/rock pools, somewhat wave-exposed shores",
        "Invasive?": "no, but can carry invasive organisms",
        "Invasive information (where, how, strategies, etc.)": "",
        "Water temperature for growth (C)": "",
        "Optimal water temperature for growth (C)": "10 to 12 (optimum growing temp, in spring)",
        "Salinity for growth (PSU)": "30-40 PSU",
        "Depth range (m)": "",
        "Is the algae facing changes with rising sea temp?": "Not likely",
        "Unnamed: 15": "Most notably impacted by changes in wave/current exposure rather than temperature change or other chnaging environmental factors ",
        "Temperature tolerance": "Intermediate. Short term acute change and long term chronic change abot to adapt and withstand temps relatively well. relatively more tolerant than most",
        "Thickness/size": "15mm",
        "Status (impacted by humans?)": "",
        "Already on the market?": "yes",
        "Listed in EU Novel Food Catalogue": "yes",
        "Food supplement at Union list of Novel Foods": "",
        "production cost (high or low and what does that mean, laborintensive, complex processing) ": "low labor and cultivation costs",
        "Can it be grown in a polyculture?": "Yes",
        "Where/how is it grown? lab/farm/wild": "mainly harvesting from wild stocks (29 companies, 10 tonnes, FAO 2022 data, EU)",
        "Growth rate + specify if in wild or cultivation (can be in terms of biomass yield) ": "rapid growth rate. optimum growth when favourable conditions occur in early spring \u2013 maximum growth rates of up to 16 mm d\u2212\"",
        "Harvesting/collection methods": "harvested by hand (France), harvested manually from shore, on foot, during low tides or from vessels by diving (Atlantic coast of Spain)",
        "Time of Harvest (incl # times a year)": "June - December",
        "processing/extraction methods": "A study used subcritical water extraction (SWE) in a pressurized reactor, using acetone fractionation to precipitate the crude fucoidan and liquid-phase. Calcium chloride was then added to the liquid phase which is conveted to sodium alginate. The yield of sodium alginate from SWE was 5.9% but increased to 15.9% with increasing acetone - greater yield than other species. New source: Hydrothermal processing until edible texture. A drying pre-treatment before boiling reduced cooking time so less leaching of antioxidants during boiling. ",
        "Country (that uses the algae)": "Sold in France, Britain, Norway, Spainm Portugal and Ireland as Sea Spaghetti for human consumption",
        "Region in europe": "Northern, Western, Southern Europe",
        "Seaboard": "",
        "Nutritional Profile": "protein (6-11%), vitamin C, Calcium, iodine, iron, magnesium, Manganese",
        "take up ": "Yes",
        "of meavy metals (Y/N)": "takes up Ammonia. New source: yes, trace amounts - Zn, boron, Fe, Manganese",
        "Companies using the algae + their product": "Dalton marine cosmetics uses in its skincare to prevent premature skin aging",
        "Industrial applications": "",
        "Extracts": "used for the extraction of carrageenans ",
        "Agriculture": "used for cultivation of artichokes",
        "Art materials + cosmetics": "used in beauty products like facial cleansers; antioxidants, moisturising, antibacterial and anti-UV properties",
        "Packaging + materials": "",
        "Environment": "",
        "Carrier of other organisms?": "Watersipora subtorquata",
        "Human consumption": "Can be dried and then used as a spagetti subsitute or soaked in water and used in Salads - mild in taste. New source: used in new meat product formulations as a natural preservative, lowers sodium content, improves phytochemical and fiber content. Considerable amounts of dietary fiber that contribute to a healthy gut + metabolic function; \" Receptacles of Himanthalia elongata can be cooked or pickled before eating them as \u201charicots de mer\u201d (sea beans). Fronds are best when collected young in spring.\"",
        "Medicinal": "Good for skin, bone. Eye, and heart health. New source: Has a wide range of bioactive compounds that contribute to properties including: anti-mycotic, anti-histamine, anticholiergic, anti-photogamage, anti-oteoperosis activities, decreasing bood cholesterol, and preventing vascular thrombosis. Contains fucoxanthin and fucoxanthinal which exert potentially anti-cancer effects, anti-inflammation, anti-obesity, anti-diabetes, anti-hypertension, anti-cardiovascular  disease, anti-microbial, antioxidant, photoreceptive, anti-angiogenesis, and anti-brain damage activities (in humans + animals)",
        "Risks + dangers": "Anyone with an iodine allergy must avoid and people with thyroid issues must consult their physicians or health care providers prior to use/consumption",
        "usage within agriculture + products": "used for the cultivation of artichokes and it is used in beauty products like facial cleansers. New source: used for the extraction of carrageenans (Atlantic coast of Spain)",
        "medicinal": "Good for skin, bone. Eye, and heart health. New source: Has a wide range of bioactive compounds that contribute to properties including: anti-mycotic, anti-histamine, anticholiergic, anti-photogamage, anti-oteoperosis activities, decreasing bood cholesterol, and preventing vascular thrombosis. Contains fucoxanthin and fucoxanthinal which exert potentially anti-cancer effects, anti-inflammation, anti-obesity, anti-diabetes, anti-hypertension, anti-cardiovascular  disease, anti-microbial, antioxidant, photoreceptive, anti-angiogenesis, and anti-brain damage activities (in humans + animals)",
        "Flavor/Diet": "Can be dried and then used as a spagetti subsitute or soaked in water and used in Salads - mild in taste. New source: used in new meat product formulations as a natural preservative, lowers sodium content, improves phytochemical and fiber content. Considerable amounts of dietary fiber that contribute to a healthy gut + metabolic function",
        "medicinal/health": "Anyone with an iodine allergy must avoid and people with thyroid issues must consult their physicians or health care providers prior to use/consumption",
        "Flavor/Diet.1": "",
        "other": "",
        "Additional properties": "",
        " ": ""
    },
