    "Ulva lactuca": {
        "Species (Latin name)": "Ulva lactuca",
        "Common name": "Sea Lettuce or Sea Salad or Atlantic Wakame",
        "Division": "Chlorophyta",
        "Micro/Macro": "Macro",
        "Color": "green",
        "Water type (marine/fresh/brackish/terrestrial)": "marine, freshwater",
        "Geographic Positioning within Europe": "barents sea, western europe, UK and Ireland, Belgium, Denmark, France, Italy, Norway",
        "Habitat": "On rock and in lower-shore rock pools, and in the shallow subtidal",
        "Invasive?": "no",
        "Invasive information (where, how, strategies, etc.)": "",
        "Water temperature for growth (C)": "14-26",
        "Optimal water temperature for growth (C)": "18",
        "Salinity for growth (PSU)": "<0.5 to 49",
        "Depth range (m)": "0 to 0.5",
        "Is the algae facing changes with rising sea temp?": "",
        "Unnamed: 15": "",
        "Temperature tolerance": "30C was lethal to species. New source: max temp 25C of species in same genus (Ulva fenestrata). New source: photosynthesis at 0 to 28C (temp range for survival)",
        "Thickness/size": "leaves are one to two cells thick",
        "Status (impacted by humans?)": "green tides from human activities, native in many european countries",
        "Already on the market?": "yes",
        "Listed in EU Novel Food Catalogue": "yes",
        "Food supplement at Union list of Novel Foods": "",
        "production cost (high or low and what does that mean, laborintensive, complex processing) ": "high cost for abtaining bioethanol compared to cane sugar etc. (1), fast growing",
        "Can it be grown in a polyculture?": "yes",
        "Where/how is it grown? lab/farm/wild": "industrial/artificial production (biorefinery) more feasible than wild collection ",
        "Growth rate + specify if in wild or cultivation (can be in terms of biomass yield) ": "more total wild collection than aquaculture (217 > 50 tonnes)(FAO 2022 data), New woruce: growth rate of up to 0.95 \u00b1 0.54% day",
        "Harvesting/collection methods": "washing up on beaches from green tides (easy because available, easily contaminated by sand + pollutants), they grow at a depth of 0-50cm. New source: harvested by hand (France), harvested manually from shore, on foot, during low tides or from vessels by diving (Atlantic coast of Spain), handpicked for high-end users such as restaurants in recent decades (Norway), Mechanical harvesting of Ulva sp. Green tides is being developed, cultivated on land-based tanks. New source: High potential for offshore aquaculture in Netherlands - native (growth season restricted to summer)",
        "Time of Harvest (incl # times a year)": "all year, largest blooms in summer so late summer/fall harvests (august, sept, oct)",
        "processing/extraction methods": "pre-treatment and/or saccharification and fermentation to be converted in bioethanol, Ulva extraction with a high yield from hot water and pressurized conditions, biorefinery (1)",
        "Country (that uses the algae)": "Great Britain, Ireland, Scandinavian Countries, China, Japan",
        "Region in europe": "Northern Europe",
        "Seaboard": "",
        "Nutritional Profile": "green algae has lower phenol content than brown (1) rich in iodine, vitamin a, vitamin b, vitamin c",
        "take up ": "Yes",
        "of meavy metals (Y/N)": "Yes, concentration is under reg limits (1)",
        "Companies using the algae + their product": "",
        "Industrial applications": "biofuel",
        "Extracts": "extraction of carrageenans",
        "Agriculture": "animal feed, enhanced vegatative growth even under drought stress, IMTA systems",
        "Art materials + cosmetics": "protect cosmetics from oxidation",
        "Packaging + materials": "",
        "Environment": "",
        "Carrier of other organisms?": "",
        "Human consumption": "commonly consumed + used in manufactured food products, used as a food supplement but not on the union list as a food supplement",
        "Medicinal": "sulfonic acids, sulphated\u00a0l-rhamnose, xylose and glucose (anti-viral, anti-tumour, anti-coagulant, anti-lipidique, hepato protective, immuno-stimulating, anti-depressant and anti-anxiolytic activities) (1), decrease risk of coronary disease, treat neurological disorders",
        "Risks + dangers": "",
        "usage within agriculture + products": "food (human + animal), biofuel, integrated multitropic aquaculutre systems, agriculutre - enhanced vegetative growth even under drought stress, protect cosmetics from oxidation (1). New source: used for the extraction of carrageenans (Atlantic coast of Spain)",
        "medicinal": "sulfonic acids, sulphated\u00a0l-rhamnose, xylose and glucose (anti-viral, anti-tumour, anti-coagulant, anti-lipidique, hepato protective, immuno-stimulating, anti-depressant and anti-anxiolytic activities) (1), decrease risk of coronary disease, treat neurological disorders",
        "Flavor/Diet": "commonly consumed, used in manufactured food products (ice cream, for example",
        "medicinal/health": "",
        "Flavor/Diet.1": "",
        "other": "",
        "Additional properties": "thermos reversible gels, carbohydrates can be a carbon source for microbial production of biomaterials (emerging market)",
        " ": ""
    },
